					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1404533&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606419180" alt="Zyn" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1404533)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/zyn" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1404533); return false;" >Zyn</a></span> <span class="odate time_1606419096 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">26 Nov 2020 19:31</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13862535/so-let-it-be-written-so-let-it-be-done">So Let It Be Written, So Let It Be Done</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4828792">
									

<p>Got a PM about this, took a look.</p>
<p>I'll admit, the hook being &quot;So let it be written, so let it be done&quot;. rather than intrigue me, makes me as a reader immediately skeptical. Written works manifesting into the real world, with accompanying (in)convenient memory-affecting effects, aren't really new or interesting so far as stories on the mainsite and popular media go (the first example that comes to mind for me is the Book of Ages from <em>Jackie Chan Adventures</em>: <a href="https://jackiechanadventures.fandom.com/wiki/Book_of_Ages">https://jackiechanadventures.fandom.com/wiki/Book_of_Ages</a> / <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aga4_I1PZKI">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aga4_I1PZKI</a>). The fact that yours is a lithostone that relies on existing written material makes this even more questionable to me as a reader; mostly because I've seen plenty of variations of the same sort of item used to bring characters and items from series like <em>Harry Potter</em> to life in the real world (and also the potential issue of readers, especially our international bilingual community, not being immediately familiar with works like Swift's <em>A Modest Proposal</em>).</p>
<p>Personally I feel like if you really want to make this work on the mainsite, you should revise it into a tale or Group of Interest format so there's more focus on the narrative behind both the bookbinders and whatever group is trying to figure out what they're doing. The core anomaly of &quot;mess with writing, real life is messed with&quot; isn't something that interests me after I've seen it attempted so many times, and what's presented here doesn't divert from the usual approach enough to convince me it'll be unique.</p>
<p>I myself feel like you'd get more positive readership if you introduce some characters whose struggles the audience can relate more with (probably the original owners of the anomaly&#8230; though ideally you'd also freshen up the worldbuilding so the acquisition of the item isn't just left to essentially dumb luck and the convenience of the item falling into the hands of someone who will conveniently use it so the effects can conveniently manifest). Then follow those characters through the story, rather than focusing on the lithostone. The Foundation itself barely features in the story anyway; I think it's better to focus on the good portions of the narrative rather than shoehorn the Foundation in. Tales like <a href="/the-orb-is-the-key">The Orb is the Key</a> and <a href="/the-worm">The Worm</a> might be a good start for guidance/inspiration.</p>

							</div>

								
					
