<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> No</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout (Optional):</strong> Mostly the usual shtick, might include interview logs and/or discovery logs. This part isn't fleshed out yet and can still change a lot.</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> All elephant graveyards are anomalous, the skeletons in them inevitably coming to live every 40-70 years. The living dead results reassemble themselves in a humanoid shape and move in a specific direction for 15 minutes before then falling apart again and repeating the process 40-70 years later.<br />
The place they are moving towards is an obscure part of Russia where other elephant skeletons have already been stacked up many meters high in an intricate shape.<br />
There also may be a secondary effect to the living skeletons that causes people to hallucinate, blurring the details of the encounter.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> No concrete narrative but rather the idea that the public is aware of the elephant skeletons, considering them an urban legend. The foundation would then have to base its procedures on that fact and make sure the people believe the scp to be a hoax.<br />
The event's age would also be explored as parallels are drawn between it and cave drawings or alleged cyclops encounters.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> The main hook would be the vague knowledge that the public has of the scp, as well as the weirdness of the event itself. Similar ideas have been made into scps before but I believe that the specifics make my concept stand out among the others.</p>
<p><strong>Additional Notes:</strong> The whole &quot;humanoid elephant skeleton&quot; idea is largely based on the theory that people used to mistake elephant skulls for those of cyclopes, creating that myth in the first place.<br />
Specific numbers and values were mostly chosen to make the scp as annoying to contain as possible(a bit silly in hindsight).<br />
I know that the idea still needs a good amount of polishing. I hope I can turn it into something sort of worthwhile in the end.</p>

