<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre:</strong> Drama/Emotional, Comedy</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout:</strong> Test and incident logs to be included. I intend to cross refence other works, but would like to ask permission from other authors before I commit to that.</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> Woman is infected with a &quot;virus&quot; of sorts that neutralizes nearby deadly threats with 'events' that work on a similar principle to the butterfly effect or a Rube Goldberg machine. It can manipulate any non-organic material to complete an event. The woman is incredulously unaware of her anomalous properties.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> The virus is contagious but this is unknown by the Foundation at the time of the article's writing. Its effects are efficient but subtle. The Subject was the child of two researchers who were killed in a containment breach. She's sent to live at the School for Children of Prominence. A boarding school/orphanage for children of Foundation personnel. She is notably idiotic and it amazes her teachers that she actually graduates. Once she starts working for the Foundation as a junior researcher, her peers are dumbfounded that despite lacking all common sense she has yet to die. This prompts an investigation and her eventual classification as an SCP. After the article is submitted for review the higher ups begin to doubt the judgement of the lead researcher and try to have the SCP declassified due to 'lack or sufficient anomalous evidence'. The lead researcher is shunned and dives into a depressed drunken stupor. It remains to be seen if the Foundation will continue the research and discover how to harness the power of this virus.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> The virus isn't all powerful and it wont infect just anyone. It's host can still be injured and it will not prevent death due to biological means such as old age or venom. It is, however, very good at keeping its host alive. The Foundation would kill to have something like this for all of their field agents.</p>

